.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt MAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm man ,
.Nm apropos ,
.Nm whatis
.Nd read a manual page, or find pages by name or subject
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl P Ar pager
.Op Ar SECTION
.Ar NAME ...
.Nm
.Fl k
.Ar WORD ...
.Nm
.Fl f
.Ar NAME ...
.Nm apropos
.Ar WORD ...
.Nm whatis
.Ar NAME ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the manual page of each
.Ar NAME
in turn, from section
.Ar SECTION
when one is given and otherwise from the first of sections 1, 8, 2, 4, 5
and 7 that has it.
The Rach2 manual begins with
.Xr rach2 7 .
.Pp
The pages are preformatted text, gzipped, under
.Pa /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N ,
rendered on the host by mandoc.
.Pa /usr/share/man/aliases
lists the names a page answers for besides its own, one
.Ql NAME SECTION TARGET
a line: every BusyBox applet is an alias of its family page, and every
member of a system-call family is an alias of the family's page.
.Nm
looks a name up as an alias first, then as a page.
.Pp
On the GNU system, a name this manual has no page for is looked up in the
same sections among the pages of the system's ports, under
.Pa /usr/share/man/gnu/catN/NAME.N ,
which the image's build renders from the ports' own sources with mandoc.
Those pages describe the upstream programs, as
.Ql man git
and
.Ql man gcc
show them.
A name both have, such as
.Ql ls ,
shows the ports' page when the command the path finds by that name is not
a link to BusyBox, since that command is the port's, and this manual's
page otherwise.
.Pp
When standard output is a terminal, the page goes through the pager named
by
.Fl P ,
then
.Ev MANPAGER ,
then
.Ev PAGER ,
then BusyBox
.Xr less 1 ,
whose display width
.Ev COLUMNS
sets; the pages are not reformatted to it.
The shell runs the pager, so it may have options, as
.Ql less -S
does.
Otherwise the page goes to standard output as it is, so
.Ql man NAME > FILE
copies the page byte for byte.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl P Ar pager
Show the pages through
.Ar pager ,
as
.Ql man -P cat ls
does, in place of
.Ev MANPAGER
and
.Ev PAGER ;
an empty
.Ar pager
shows the page as it is.
.It Fl w
Print the path of each page that would be shown instead of the page.
.It Fl k
Print the line of every page whose names or summary hold one of the
.Ar WORD Ns s ,
in any case:
.Ql NAME (SECTION) - SUMMARY ,
for each name of the page that holds the word, or for its first name when
only the summary does.
This is
.Nm apropos .
.It Fl f
Print the line of every page that has one of the
.Ar NAME Ns s
among its names.
This is
.Nm whatis .
.El
.Pp
The lines are read from the NAME section each page begins with, on every
call, so there is no index to keep current; a word or a name no page has
prints
.Ql WORD: nothing appropriate
on standard error.
On the GNU system
.Nm apropos
and
.Nm whatis
are links to
.Nm ;
elsewhere they are
.Fl k
and
.Fl f .
.Pp
.Nm
is a shell script of this project.
BusyBox's man applet is not built: it finds a page only as a file, and a
file for each of the hundreds of aliases would put that many nodes on
every root, which the RAM root and a KRFFS disk walk on every lookup.
.Pp
The manual sections are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 1
Programs.
.It 2
System calls.
.It 4
Devices.
.It 5
File formats.
.It 7
Overviews.
.It 8
Administration.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N
.It Pa /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N
Installed preformatted pages.
.It Pa /usr/share/man/aliases
The names a page answers for.
.It Pa /usr/share/man/gnu/catN/NAME.N
The pages of the GNU system's ports.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is 0, or 1 when a pager failed or a name, or for
.Fl k
and
.Fl f
a word, found no page, and 2 for no name or an option it does not know,
which prints the usage.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl man rach2
.Dl man 2 open
.Dl man ls
.Dl man -w 2 open
.Dl man -k socket
.Dl whatis sync
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr less 1 ,
.Xr rach2 7
